WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.604
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.604 --> 00:00:04.699
- [Narrator] This is the Lord
Howe Island stick insect,

00:00:04.699 --> 00:00:07.504
and this species did the impossible,

00:00:07.504 --> 00:00:10.352
they came back from extinction.

00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:12.225
The Lord Howe Island stick insect

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:14.509
is the largest flightless stick insect,

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:17.603
and can grow up to four inches in length.

00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:20.894
That's almost the length
of an adult human's hand.

00:00:20.894 --> 00:00:23.621
It is affectionately
nicknamed the tree lobster,

00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:27.726
the land lobster, and
even the walking sausage.

00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:29.518
Lord Howe Island stick insects

00:00:29.518 --> 00:00:31.964
were once so common on Lord Howe Island

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:34.603
that they were used as bait by fishermen.

00:00:34.603 --> 00:00:38.729
Then a British supply ship
crashed on the island in 1918,

00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:43.275
accidentally introducing
rats who ate the insects.

00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:46.275
By 1960, they were declared extinct.

00:00:47.188 --> 00:00:50.975
Then in 2001, Australian
scientists discovered

00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:55.142
a tiny population hidden on a
small volcanic island nearby.

00:00:56.143 --> 00:00:58.706
Thanks to a breeding
program at Melbourne Zoo,

00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:02.728
they have been able to save
and repopulate this species.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:05.821
This is the Lord Howe Island stick insect.

00:01:05.821 --> 00:01:09.023
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.023 --> 00:01:11.523
(tone sounds)

